.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 151 PM PST Thu Nov 19 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

Rain showers persist this afternoon across the coastal portions
of the Bay Area and all of the Central Coast. There remains a
slight chance of thunderstorms across these areas through early
afternoon before the greatest instability shifts inland across the
Central Valley. That said, there is a potential for gusty winds
within any heavier rain shower or thunderstorm that does develop.
There is also a non-zero chance of waterspouts through early
afternoon especially along the aforementioned areas where we
maintain minimal insatiability. The greatest insatiability will
shift inland later through this evening with the thunderstorm
threat diminishing across the Bay Area and Central Coast as the
surface low shifts farther inland.

For tonight, we have Cold Weather Advisory in effect from 1 AM to
8 AM Friday as temperatures appear low enough for portions of the
North Bay, East Bay and Central Coast, where MinT will fall
between 33 and 36 degrees. There is an increasing potential for
cloud cover to limit the cooling across these regions and the Cold
Weather Advisory may need to be canceled (or portions of it) for
tonight/Friday morning. Snow levels are currently forecast to be
below 5,000 ft, so we can see a mix of rain/snow in the regions
highest peaks in the Santa Lucia Range.

There remain chances for rain showers to persist over the Pacific
and adjacent inland areas through tomorrow afternoon, yet the
thunderstorm threat is less than 5%. Temperatures on Friday
afternoon will be some 5 to 10 degrees below average for mid/late
November with periods of sunshine.

Friday night/Saturday morning is forecast to be another cold one
with Cold Weather Advisories in places for interior portions of
our region. However, as another frontal system is forecast to move
into the region, there remains a great deal of uncertainty of how
cold our interior valleys will get. That said, it still is
forecast to be the coldest morning of the week.

