.LONG TERM...
Issued at 151 PM PST Thu Nov 19 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

Late Saturday morning our next frontal boundary is forecast to
move through the North Bay and then begin to dissipate as is moves
southward across the Central Coast. Thus, we are no longer
expecting widespread rainfall across the region as the mid/upper
level low stays well to the north off of the Pacific
Northwest/British Columbia coast. That said, this weekend won`t be
dry with post frontal rain showers possible through late Sunday
night and into Monday mourning. Monday afternoon, much of the
region is forecast to be dry with temperatures still remaining
below normal.

Longer term guidance continues to suggest the storm door remains
open as with a series of upper level troughs moving through the
Bay Area and Central Coast. The next system currently appears to
arrive Tuesday through Wednesday with increasing potential for
widespread rain (potential for 1-3" of rain across the North Bay
and the coastal ranges). However, there are still some differences
in the timing and placement of important synoptic scale features
that prevent us from getting in to many details at this time.
However, given the time of year, there could be some noteworthy
impacts for those traveling. Be sure to remain weather aware and
check back for additional details as we move forward in time.

